s1: 1 <composition>
	why=Reading text aloud needs the same chunking and block-finding logic whichever engine speaks it, and locating a reader-chosen start point needs the same kind of pure text math.
	build=Text is split into speakable sentence chunks, EPUB/PDF content is turned into an ordered list of readable pieces, and a selection or a tap is turned into a sentence index inside one of those pieces.
	evidence=Each helper is a pure function with a doctest-style example in its own docstring.
	code=|
		- [`splitSentences`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=splitSentences kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_1: 1.1 Split text into sentence chunks <leaf>
		why=A screen reader speaks better in sentence-sized pieces than one long paragraph, and no engine here chunks its input for the caller.
		description=Splits on sentence-ending punctuation, then hard-wraps any piece still longer than 280 characters at the nearest space.
		build=`splitSentences(text, max)` returns an ordered array of chunks no longer than `max`.
		evidence=Its docstring example turns `"One. Two!  Three?"` into `['One.', 'Two!', 'Three?']`.
		code=|
			- [`splitSentences`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=splitSentences kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_2: 1.2 Collect readable DOM blocks <leaf>
		why=`playEpub` (4.3) needs one flat, ordered list of the paragraph-like elements in an EPUB chapter, with nested duplicates removed.
		description=Selects `p,h1..h6,li,blockquote,dt,dd,figcaption,pre,td,th` and drops a block that contains another block, so nested text is read once.
		build=`collectBlocks(doc)` returns the leaf block elements of a document in reading order.
		evidence=Called from `playEpub` (4.3) on the EPUB iframe document; a manual EPUB read-aloud pass is the user's acceptance check.
		code=|
			- [`collectBlocks`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=collectBlocks kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_3: 1.3 Locate the block at an anchor <leaf>
		why=Reading must start from the block the reader can currently see, not from the top of the chapter.
		description=Returns the index of the first block at or after a given DOM node, using document position when the node is not itself a block.
		build=`blockIndexFrom(blocks, node)` returns `-1` when `blocks` is empty and no block follows `node`.
		evidence=Feeds `playEpub` (4.3), which starts its walk from `reader._visibleAnchorEl()`.
		code=|
			- [`blockIndexFrom`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=blockIndexFrom kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_4: 1.4 Turn PDF text items into text <leaf>
		why=`pdf.js` returns text as a list of positioned items, not a string; `splitSentences` (1.1) needs a string.
		description=Joins each item's `str`, inserting a newline after an item whose `hasEOL` flag is set and a space otherwise.
		build=`pdfItemsToText(items)` returns one string per page, ready for `splitSentences`.
		evidence=Feeds `playPdf` (4.4), one call per rendered PDF page.
		code=|
			- [`pdfItemsToText`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=pdfItemsToText kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_5: 1.5 Locate the sentence at a text offset <leaf>
		why=`playFrom` (4.5) must skip the sentences already spoken before a chosen start point, not restart the whole block.
		description=Splits the block's text the same way `splitSentences` (1.1) does, then walks the resulting chunks until the given character offset falls inside one.
		build=`sentenceIndexAt(text, offset)` returns the index of the `splitSentences()` chunk that holds `offset`.
		evidence=Its docstring example turns `('One. Two! Three?', 6)` into `1`.
		code=|
			- [`sentenceIndexAt`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=sentenceIndexAt kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_6: 1.6 Convert a DOM point to a character offset <leaf>
		why=A selection or a caret lookup gives a `(node, offset)` DOM point; `sentenceIndexAt` (1.5) needs a plain character offset counted from the start of the block's own text.
		description=Builds a `Range` from the start of the block element to `(node, offset)` and measures its stringified length; returns `0` if the `Range` cannot be built.
		build=`textOffsetIn(el, node, offset)` returns the character offset of `(node, offset)` inside `el.textContent`.
		evidence=Feeds `playEpub` (4.3) when a start point is given, and `playFrom` (4.5) indirectly through it.
		code=|
			- [`textOffsetIn`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=textOffsetIn kind=Function focus=changed}
	n1_7: 1.7 Locate the DOM point under a tap <leaf>
		why=`playFromPoint` (4.6) only has viewport coordinates from a touch event; the sentence helpers (1.5, 1.6) need a DOM point.
		description=Uses `caretRangeFromPoint` where the runtime supports it, else `caretPositionFromPoint`; returns `null` when neither does.
		build=`caretAt(doc, x, y)` returns `{node, offset}` or `null`.
		assumption=Unknown (needs your test): `caretRangeFromPoint`/`caretPositionFromPoint` support was not verified across every browser this reader targets.
		code=|
			- [`caretAt`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=caretAt kind=Function focus=changed}
s2: 2 <composition>
	why=Android's WebView has no `window.speechSynthesis`, so speaking on Android needs the platform's own `TextToSpeech` reached through a JavaScript bridge.
	build=A `JsBridge` method starts or stops speech, a lazily created `TextToSpeech` instance speaks one utterance at a time, and its result is reported back into the page.
	evidence=`window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak`/`ttsStop` exist on the bridge and every utterance resolves to exactly one `window.__codexaTtsEvent` call.
	assumption=Unknown (needs your test): this Kotlin change has not been compiled or run; confirm the app builds and speaks on a device before relying on it.
	code=|
		- [`MainActivity`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=worktree show=lines lines=209:270}
	n2_1: 2.1 Declare TTS_SERVICE package visibility <leaf>
		why=Android 11+ hides other packages from an app unless it declares which intents it needs to query, and `TextToSpeech` must find an installed engine package.
		description=Adds a `<queries>` block naming the `android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE` intent action.
		build=The manifest grants visibility into installed TTS engines on API 30+.
		evidence=Without it, `TextToSpeech(this){...}` can silently find no engine on a fresh API 30+ install.
		code=|
			- [`AndroidManifest.xml`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml){change=worktree show=lines lines=7:12}
	n2_2: 2.2 Expose ttsSpeak/ttsStop to JavaScript <leaf>
		why=`androidEngine` (3.1) in the page needs a synchronous-looking call into native code, and Android's JS bridge only accepts primitive arguments and returns nothing.
		description=`@JavascriptInterface` methods that hop to the UI thread and call `speakTts`/stop the current utterance.
		build=`ttsSpeak(id, text, rate, lang)` and `ttsStop()` are callable as `window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak(...)` from `androidEngine` (3.1).
		evidence=`androidEngine.speak` calls `bridge.ttsSpeak` inside a try/catch and resolves `'error'` if the call throws.
		code=|
			- [`ttsSpeak`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=worktree show=lines lines=209:213}
			- [`ttsStop`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=worktree show=lines lines=215:218}
	n2_3: 2.3 Speak with lazy init and one pending utterance <leaf>
		why=Creating a `TextToSpeech` engine is asynchronous and should happen once, not on every utterance; a call that arrives before init finishes must not be dropped.
		description=Speaks immediately once the engine is ready; otherwise replaces any earlier pending utterance (reporting it `stopped`) and starts the engine at most once.
		build=`speakTts(id, text, rate, lang)` keeps at most one queued utterance and lazily owns the `TextToSpeech` instance.
		evidence=A second call while the engine is still initializing reports the first call's id as `stopped`, matching the "one pending utterance" comment above it.
		code=|
			- [`speakTts`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=worktree show=lines lines=230:244}
	n2_4: 2.4 Finish init and listen for utterance progress <leaf>
		why=`speak()` only reports through callbacks; the bridge (2.2) needs each callback turned into exactly one event per utterance id.
		description=On successful init, plays the one pending utterance and installs an `UtteranceProgressListener` that maps `onDone`/`onError`/`onStop` to `sendTtsEvent` (2.5). On failure, shuts the engine down and reports `error`.
		build=`onTtsInit(status)` leaves `tts` either usable and listened-to, or `null`.
		evidence=`sendTtsEvent` fires exactly once per utterance id, from whichever branch of the listener the engine takes.
		code=|
			- [`onTtsInit`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=worktree show=lines lines=246:265}
	n2_5: 2.5 Report an utterance event back to JavaScript <leaf>
		why=`androidEngine` (3.1) resolves its `speak()` promise from `window.__codexaTtsEvent`, so the native side needs a way to call back into the page.
		description=Builds a small JS call with the utterance id and event name and runs it through `WebView.evaluateJavascript`.
		build=`sendTtsEvent(id, event)` calls `window.__codexaTtsEvent(id, event)` in the page, guarded by `&&` so a page without the handler does nothing.
		evidence=`androidEngine`'s `pending` map (3.1) resolves the matching promise the moment this call lands.
		code=|
			- [`sendTtsEvent`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=worktree show=lines lines=267:270}
	n2_6: 2.6 Release TextToSpeech on destroy <leaf>
		why=A `TextToSpeech` engine holds a system-service connection that leaks if the activity is destroyed while it is open.
		description=Shuts the engine down and clears the reference before the WebView itself is destroyed.
		build=`onDestroy()` calls `tts?.shutdown()` ahead of its existing WebView teardown.
		evidence=No test exercises `onDestroy`; the user's own manual app-close check is the evidence for this leaf.
		code=|
			- [`onDestroy`](../../../../Android/app/src/main/java/com/codexa/reader/MainActivity.kt){change=worktree show=lines lines=322:324}
s3: 3 <composition>
	why=Two runtimes can speak: Android WebView through a native bridge, and iOS WKWebView/browsers through `speechSynthesis`. The controller (4) should not know which one it has.
	build=Both engines expose the same `speak(text, {rate, lang}) -> Promise<'done'|'stopped'|'error'>` and `stop()` shape; `pickEngine` chooses one at controller creation.
	evidence=`createTts` (4) calls `pickEngine()` once and never branches on engine name again.
	code=|
		- [`pickEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=pickEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
	n3_1: 3.1 Android engine adapter <leaf>
		why=`window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak` resolves through an out-of-band callback (2.5), not a return value, so it needs wrapping into the shared Promise-based engine shape.
		description=Keeps a `pending` map from utterance id to its `resolve` function; `window.__codexaTtsEvent` (set here) looks the id up and resolves it.
		build=`androidEngine(win, bridge).speak(text, opts)` resolves once `sendTtsEvent` (2.5) reports that utterance's id.
		evidence=`stop()` resolves every still-pending promise as `'stopped'` before clearing the map, so a stopped engine never leaves a caller waiting.
		code=|
			- [`androidEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=androidEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
	n3_2: 3.2 Web engine adapter <leaf>
		why=iOS WKWebView and desktop/mobile browsers expose `window.speechSynthesis` directly; no native bridge is needed there.
		description=Wraps one `SpeechSynthesisUtterance` per call; `unlock()` speaks an empty utterance so iOS treats the first real utterance as inside the same user gesture.
		build=`webEngine(synth).speak(text, opts)` resolves `'done'`, or `'stopped'` for an interrupted/canceled utterance, or `'error'` otherwise.
		assumption=Unknown (needs your test): whether iOS's silent (mute) switch silences `speechSynthesis` inside a WKWebView is unconfirmed on-device.
		code=|
			- [`webEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=webEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
	n3_3: 3.3 Pick an engine for this runtime <leaf>
		why=The controller (4) is created once per rendition and must not re-detect the runtime on every utterance.
		description=Prefers `window.AndroidCodexa.ttsSpeak` when present, then `window.speechSynthesis`, else no engine.
		build=`pickEngine(win)` returns an engine object or `null`; `createTts` (4.1) treats `null` as "read-aloud unavailable".
		evidence=`_tts.available` (used by 5.2's header-button visibility) is exactly `!!engine` from this pick.
		code=|
			- [`pickEngine`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=pickEngine kind=Function focus=changed}
s4: 4 <composition>
	why=One controller owns the play/stop state, the block-by-block or page-by-page read, the rule for when a page turn is allowed, and where in the book a run starts.
	build=`createTts(reader, opts)` returns `{available, playing, play, stop, toggle, onRelocated, playFrom, playFromPoint}` bound to one `CXReader` instance.
	evidence=Toggling `btn-tts` (5.2) starts and stops audible speech with the highlighted block tracking the page; selecting text or tapping while reading jumps that same run to a chosen sentence.
	code=|
		- [`createTts`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus=changed}
	n4_1: 4.1 Play, stop, and toggle state <leaf>
		why=One run must be cleanly cancellable mid-sentence, mid-block, or mid-page, without a stale `await` finishing late and resuming speech nobody asked for.
		description=A `run` counter is bumped on every `stop()`/`play()`; every loop and `await` in 4.3/4.4 checks its own captured `run` value before acting again.
		build=`play(start)` picks `playPdf` or `playEpub(myRun, start)` by `reader._isPdf`/`_isCbz`; `stop()` bumps `run`, clears the highlight, and calls `engine.stop()`; `toggle()` picks between them from `playing`.
		evidence=A `stop()` immediately followed by `play()` (as `onRelocated`, 4.2, does) never leaves two runs speaking at once, because the older run's `myRun === run` check fails on its next step.
		code=|
			- [`createTts` play/stop/toggle](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function play(start) {]" context=8}
	n4_2: 4.2 Restart reading after a user page turn <leaf>
		why=A page turn the reader makes themselves should not be silently ignored, and a page turn this module makes to keep pace should not restart the same block.
		description=Ignores `cx-relocated` while not playing, while `selfTurn` is true, or within 600ms after this module's own last turn; otherwise stops and replays from the new visible block.
		build=`onRelocated()` is the whole rule; `turn(fn)` (used by 4.3/4.4) is what sets `selfTurn`/`selfTurnUntil` around this module's own `reader.next()`/`goToSpineItem()` calls.
		invariant=TTS never turns a page the reader did not ask for except to keep the spoken block on screen; a reader-driven page turn restarts reading from the newly visible page.
		decision=Restarting from the new page trades mid-sentence continuity for a simple rule: no resume position is tracked across a manual turn.
		evidence=Acceptance example: open an EPUB in the Android app, tap read-aloud, hear the first visible paragraph, and see the page turn only when speech reaches the next page.
		code=|
			- [`onRelocated`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[onRelocated() {]" context=6}
			- [`turn` self-turn guard](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function turn(fn) {]" context=4}
	n4_3: 4.3 Read an EPUB block by block <leaf>
		why=An EPUB chapter is longer than one screen, so reading it must walk blocks across page turns and, in continuous mode, across appended chapters; and it must be able to start mid-chapter at a chosen point instead of always at the visible anchor.
		description=Starts from `start.node` (4.5) when given, else the visible anchor (1.3); when `start` lands inside a block, skips the sentences before it using `sentenceIndexAt`/`textOffsetIn` (1.5, 1.6). Otherwise finds the next block from the current index, turns pages under the guard (4.2) until it is visible, highlights it with `cx-tts-active`, and speaks its sentences (1.1) in order; re-collects blocks (1.2) at a chapter or continuous-append boundary, falling back to the visible anchor (1.3) when the last spoken block is no longer connected to the document.
		build=`playEpub(myRun, start)` runs until `myRun !== run` (4.1), an error, or the end of the book.
		evidence=Acceptance example: the highlighted paragraph advances one at a time and the page turns only when the next block is off screen.
		assumption=Unknown: whether continuous (scrolled) mode ever detaches the previously read block from the document is still unverified against every continuous-mode re-render path; the `last.isConnected` check only decides which of two resume strategies runs, not whether either one lands on the exact sentence the reader expects.
		code=|
			- [`playEpub`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function playEpub(myRun, start) {]" context=20}
			- [`showBlock` page-turn loop](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function showBlock(el, myRun) {]" context=8}
	n4_4: 4.4 Read a PDF page by page <leaf>
		why=A PDF has no DOM blocks; its text comes from `pdf.js` one page at a time, and a scanned page has no text layer at all.
		description=Walks `reader.spine` from the current page, reading one or two pages per spread (1.4, 1.1); stops and reports no readable text after 3 consecutive empty pages.
		build=`playPdf(myRun)` runs until `myRun !== run` (4.1), an error, no-text, or the end of the spine.
		evidence=`opts.onNoText()` fires only after 3 consecutive pages with zero sentences, so one blank page does not stop the read.
		code=|
			- [`playPdf`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[async function playPdf(myRun) {]" context=20}
	n4_5: 4.5 Jump reading to a chosen point <leaf>
		why=A reader-chosen start point (a selection or a tap) should win over wherever the visible-page anchor happens to be, matching the start-here invariant.
		description=Stops any run in progress, then calls `play` (4.1) with `{node, offset}` so `playEpub` (4.3) starts from that point instead of the visible anchor; a no-op for PDF/CBZ renditions or when no node is given, since only EPUB has DOM blocks to jump inside.
		build=`playFrom(node, offset)` calls `stop()` then `play({node, offset})`.
		invariant=A user-chosen start point wins over the visible-page anchor: `playEpub` (4.3) uses `start.node` instead of `reader._visibleAnchorEl()` whenever `playFrom` supplies one.
		evidence=`playEpub` (4.3) reads `start?.node` ahead of `reader._visibleAnchorEl?.()` in its own `blockIndexFrom` call.
		code=|
			- [`playFrom`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[playFrom(node, offset) {]" context=4}
	n4_6: 4.6 Jump reading to a tapped point <leaf>
		why=The touch handler (5.9) only has viewport coordinates from the tap, not a DOM point that `playFrom` (4.5) needs.
		description=Resolves the coordinates to a DOM point with `caretAt` (1.7), confirms the point lands inside a readable block, then delegates to `playFrom` (4.5); returns `false` instead of acting when there is no text under the point, so the caller can fall through to its own handling.
		build=`playFromPoint(doc, x, y)` returns `false` when no text block is under the point, otherwise calls `playFrom` and returns `true`.
		invariant=The tap jump never overrides the left/right page-turn zones: the touch handler (5.9) only calls `playFromPoint` after those zones are checked, and `playFromPoint`'s own `false` return lets an off-text tap fall through unclaimed.
		evidence=A tap outside any readable block returns `false`, which the touch handler (5.9) reads as "let this tap continue to the vertical nav zones".
		code=|
			- [`playFromPoint`](../../../../public/js/tts.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=createTts kind=Function focus="match[playFromPoint(doc, x, y) {]" context=6}
s5: 5 <composition>
	why=A controller with no button, no label, and no lifecycle wiring is code nobody can reach from the reading screen.
	build=A labeled header button toggles the controller, a settings row exposes read-aloud speed, reading stops wherever a rendition already ends or restarts, and a selection or a tap can jump reading to a chosen point.
	evidence=`btn-tts` is visible except for CBZ books or when no engine exists, speech stops before the reader can leave the page it was reading, and both the annotation-toolbar action and the tap gesture reach `playFrom`/`playFromPoint` (4.5, 4.6).
	code=|
		- [`reader.js` TTS wiring](../../../../public/js/reader.js){change=worktree show=lines lines=6725:6737}
	n5_1: 5.1 Read-aloud reader-facing text <leaf>
		why=The button, its settings row, the two runtime toasts, and the annotation-toolbar action all need STE-100 labels before anything can reference them.
		description=Adds `reader.btn_tts`, `reader.header_btn_tts`, `reader.tts_rate`, `reader.tts_error`, `reader.tts_no_text`, and `reader.annotation_tts_here`.
		build=Six new keys under `public/locales/en.json`.
		evidence=Every other leaf in this module reads one of these keys by id; none of them writes English text inline.
		code=|
			- [`en.json` read-aloud strings](../../../../public/locales/en.json){change=worktree show=lines lines=812:817}
	n5_2: 5.2 Header button and its visibility rule <leaf>
		why=The reader needs one button to start and stop listening, and it must disappear where it cannot work (CBZ has no text; no engine means no speech).
		description=Adds `#btn-tts` to the header row and hides it unless the preference is on, an engine was found (3.3), and the open book is not a CBZ.
		build=`applyHeaderBtnVisibility()` sets `btn-tts`'s visibility from `prefs.headerBtnTts && !!_tts?.available && !_cxReader?._isCbz`.
		evidence=Opening a CBZ, or a runtime with no speech engine, leaves the button hidden even when the preference is on.
		code=|
			- [`btn-tts` markup](../../../../public/reader.html){change=worktree show=lines lines=234:234}
			- [`applyHeaderBtnVisibility`](../../../../public/js/reader.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=applyHeaderBtnVisibility kind=Function focus=changed}
	n5_3: 5.3 Settings panel: header toggle and rate slider <leaf>
		why=The reader needs to hide the button entirely and to slow down or speed up speech without leaving the book.
		description=A toggle row persists `prefs.headerBtnTts`; a `0.5`-`2.0` range input persists `prefs.ttsRate`, read by the controller's `getRate` (4.1) on every utterance.
		build=`syncSettingsUi`/`initSettingsUi` read and write `header-btn-tts-toggle` and `tts-rate-slider`; `#btn-tts.tts-playing` gives the button a pressed look while speaking.
		evidence=Dragging the slider updates `tts-rate-value` immediately and the next spoken sentence uses the new rate.
		code=|
			- [settings rows](../../../../public/reader.html){change=worktree show=lines lines=597:609}
			- [`syncSettingsUi` TTS rows](../../../../public/js/reader.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=syncSettingsUi kind=Function focus="match[const hbtt = document.getElementById]" context=6}
			- [`initSettingsUi` rate slider](../../../../public/js/reader.js){change=worktree show=symbol symbol=initSettingsUi kind=Function focus="match[document.getElementById('tts-rate-slider')]" context=5}
			- [`.tts-playing` style](../../../../public/css/reader.css){change=worktree show=lines lines=381:381}
	n5_4: 5.4 Create the controller and wire its callbacks <leaf>
		why=`createTts` (4) needs a live `CXReader`, the current rate/language, and a way to reach the header button and toasts; this leaf is where those seams meet.
		description=Builds the controller right after `_cxReader` is constructed, passing `getRate`, `getLang` (from EPUB metadata language), `onState` (toggles `.tts-playing` and `aria-pressed`), `onError`, and `onNoText`; the button's click handler calls `toggle()`.
		build=`_tts = createTts(_cxReader, {...})` inside `startCXRendition`; a `click` listener on `btn-tts` calls `_tts?.toggle()`.
		evidence=Tapping the button while a book is open flips `.tts-playing` on `btn-tts` in step with `onState`.
		code=|
			- [controller creation](../../../../public/js/reader.js){change=worktree show=lines lines=6725:6737}
			- [click handler](../../../../public/js/reader.js){change=worktree show=lines lines=8096:8096}
	n5_5: 5.5 Stop reading at every rendition boundary <leaf>
		why=Speech left running past the page, sleep timer, or library return it started in would keep talking over a screen the reader no longer sees as the active book.
		description=Calls `_tts?.stop()` before closing a rendition, before the dim/lock action of a fired sleep timer, and when returning to the library; clears `_tts` before a new rendition replaces `_cxReader`.
		build=Three call sites plus the `_tts = null` reset in `startCXRendition`.
		assumption=Unknown (needs your test): Android's WebView JavaScript keeps running after `onPause`, so a locked screen may not stop speech on its own; iOS needs `UIBackgroundModes=audio` for background playback, which this change does not add.
		evidence=Locking the sleep timer's dim overlay or leaving the reader silences any speech in progress.
		code=|
